    - Ken Burns (Slow Pan & Zoom): Gently scales and pans a high-res photo over several seconds. It gives still landscapes and cityscapes a cinematic "living postcard" feel while showcasing fine detail. Keep motion slow (around 6–10 seconds) and scale modest (roughly 1.05x to 1.12x) so high-res images stay sharp and never feel jittery.
    - Smooth Crossfade (Dissolve): Fades one image out while fading the next in over 600–1,000 ms with an ease-in-out curve. Ideal for hero banners, mood shifts (such as day to dusk), and editorial travel stories where hard cuts feel abrupt.
    - Subtle Parallax Slide: Moves the incoming and outgoing images at slightly different speeds or offsets as they slide horizontally or vertically. This creates a sense of depth and spatial journey, especially when paired with destination titles or route cards.
    - Reveal Mask / Wipe (Soft Edge): Uncovers the next photo using a soft gradient wipe or subtle scale-up reveal rather than a harsh mechanical slice. Works well for modern destination showcases when kept gentle and unhurried.


